From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pve-edge-kernel <pve-edge-kernel@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:45:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] x86/cfi: Only poison real kCFI and FineIBT preambles

apply_seal_endbr() calls poison_cfi() for every function in
.ibt_endbr_seal, which objtool also generates for code that was not
compiled with kCFI, such as binary blobs linked into out-of-tree
modules. poison_cfi() takes the 16 bytes in front of such a function for
a kCFI preamble if the first byte is a MOV imm32 opcode, and for a
FineIBT preamble if it is an ENDBR. In such code, these bytes are the
tail of the preceding function, which then gets its MOV immediate or
its ENDBR and the following instruction overwritten.

With the NVIDIA vGPU host driver 595.91.04 on a kCFI kernel, this
corrupted 525 functions of its precompiled resource manager, most of
them by replacing the return value in "mov $1, %eax", and GPU
initialization failed with RmInitAdapter 0x22:0x40 (NV_ERR_INVALID_STATE)
without any CFI failure. With FineIBT, 1399 functions of the same
module would be hit.

Also check the NOP padding of kCFI preambles, and the SUBL following
the ENDBR of FineIBT preambles.

---
 arch/x86/kernel/alternative.c | 15 +++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/alternative.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/alternative.c
@@ -1921,9 +1921,11 @@
 		addr += CFI_OFFSET - fineibt_prefix_size;
 
 		/*
-		 * FineIBT prefix should start with an ENDBR.
+		 * FineIBT prefix should start with an ENDBR, followed by the
+		 * SUBL of the hash. An ENDBR alone might as well be the start
+		 * of a preceding function, in code not compiled with kCFI.
 		 */
-		if (!is_endbr(addr))
+		if (!is_endbr(addr) || *(u8 *)(addr + fineibt_preamble_hash - 1) != 0x2d)
 			break;
 
 		/*
@@ -1940,9 +1942,14 @@
 
 	case CFI_KCFI:
 		/*
-		 * kCFI prefix should start with a valid hash.
+		 * kCFI prefix should start with a valid hash, followed by NOP
+		 * padding. Without checking the padding, the tail of a preceding
+		 * function in code not compiled with kCFI, e.g. a binary blob
+		 * linked into an out-of-tree module, could match and get
+		 * scribbled over.
 		 */
-		if (!decode_preamble_hash(addr, NULL))
+		if (!decode_preamble_hash(addr, NULL) ||
+		    (CFI_OFFSET > 5 && memchr_inv(addr + 5, 0x90, CFI_OFFSET - 5)))
 			break;
 
 		/*
